WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.110
(water whooshing)
(soft gentle music)

00:00:02.110 --> 00:00:03.160
- In the United States

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:05.750
we're facing a national water shortage.

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:07.210
Government backed research shows

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:08.910
that in a little over of 50 years,

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:12.425
half of the freshwater basins
may not meet our demands.

00:00:12.425 --> 00:00:14.983
(water rippling)
(soft gentle music)

00:00:14.983 --> 00:00:17.857
For this story I'm in my
home state of Florida.

00:00:17.857 --> 00:00:20.220
Here, the water crisis is real

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.630
and demanding positive action.

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:24.400
(birds tweeting)
(soft gentle music)

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:28.070
This is Silver Springs, almost
half of Florida's freshwater

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:29.698
comes from aquifers like this.

00:00:29.698 --> 00:00:31.623
Natural, underground reservoirs

00:00:31.623 --> 00:00:33.787
that are topped up by rainfall.

00:00:33.787 --> 00:00:35.010
(soft gentle music)

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:36.290
But earlier this year,

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:40.010
wet season rainfall in the
south of the state fell by 45%.

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:41.550
And the pressure is mounting,

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:43.240
as Florida's population grows

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:45.650
by around 300,000 people a year.

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:48.583
Our water situation is unsustainable.

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:52.780
I've come to Maples Botanical Garden,

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:54.320
a leader in best practices

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:57.200
for conserving water and hoard of culture.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:58.240
If I understand correctly,

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:00.111
up to half of Florida's residential water

00:01:00.111 --> 00:01:02.600
goes back into irrigating our gardens.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:04.890
What can we do to make
that more efficient.

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:06.240
- We have about six months out of the year

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.150
where we have an abundance of rainfall

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:10.287
and six months where we
have drought conditions.

00:01:10.287 --> 00:01:11.950
Being a botanical garden,

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:13.973
we really focus on selecting plants

00:01:13.973 --> 00:01:16.244
that can take those cycles of drought.

00:01:16.244 --> 00:01:18.989
If you do that we can really
cut down on irrigation.

00:01:18.989 --> 00:01:21.438
That's really climate friendly gardening.

00:01:21.438 --> 00:01:23.413
We're working with nature.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:26.320
- Is there something we can do at home

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:28.740
to collect our water, protect
our water and reuse it.

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:31.230
- Yeah, so in my own
home I have rain barrels.

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:32.930
When it rains we collect water

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:35.350
and I use that water to water my garden,

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:36.771
throughout the dry months of the year.

00:01:36.771 --> 00:01:39.120
Quite often we think of the environment

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:40.490
as something that's out there.

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:41.890
We think of the Everglades,

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:44.750
but really the things
we do in our own homes,

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:45.830
that's apart of the environment.

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:47.830
Our backyard is part of the environment.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:51.800
- But it's not just outside
where we can make a difference.

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:53.860
On average, American
families use more than

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:56.050
300 gallons of water everyday.

00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:58.370
And a lot of that is being wasted.

00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:00.820
I'd love to see how my family
could save water at home.

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:02.387
- Just in time America.

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:06.400
- From dripping toilets or
faulty facets leaks in our homes

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:09.380
can loose nearly a trillion
gallons of water every year.

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:11.300
Just replacing a 50 cent washer

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:15.050
on a faucet can save up to
3,000 gallons of water per year.

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:16.290
And many of us still

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:18.300
rinse our dirty dishes
under a running faucet

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:21.050
before loading them into the dishwasher.

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:22.635
This is a huge waste of water.

00:02:22.635 --> 00:02:25.520
Because you actually need
the dishes to be dirty

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:27.290
as the enzymes in the detergent

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:29.803
latch on to food particles
to work effectively.

00:02:31.490 --> 00:02:33.160
We have less water than we think.

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:36.120
But the minute we realize
that every drop is valuable,

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:38.570
we begin to change our
behavior for the better.

00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:41.280
Whether that's creating a
climate friendly garden,

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:42.980
fixing that leaky faucet,

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:44.950
or just not pre-rinsing our dishes.

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:47.070
It's these little water saving measures

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:48.740
that can be done by me in the home

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:50.136
and all of us across America.

00:02:50.136 --> 00:02:52.165
That can help make a huge difference

00:02:52.165 --> 00:02:54.009
for our future water security.

00:02:54.009 --> 00:02:57.009
(soft gentle music)

